[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0mDone. [38;5;10mtranslation/[0m builds clean with [38;5;10mcargo build --release[0m and is byte-identical to the C reference across 48 differential test cases (stdout, stderr, exit status, and files written).[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Layout, mirroring the C:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10msrc/cio.rs[0m — C stdio emulation: one byte stream for stdin shared by [38;5;10mfgets[0m/[38;5;10mscanf[0m/[38;5;10mgetchar[0m (so [38;5;10mscanf[0m reads across newlines while [38;5;10mfgets[0m stops at one), glibc-style stdout buffering (4096-byte blocks when redirected, line buffered on a terminal), unbuffered stderr, and a [38;5;10mprintf[0m renderer for [38;5;10m%s[0m/[38;5;10m%d[0m/[38;5;10m%p[0m that works on raw bytes.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10msrc/shape.rs[0m — the ten singleton shapes with art copied verbatim; pointer identity becomes index identity.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10msrc/scene.rs[0m — scene create/add/remove/print/equals/save/load/list.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10msrc/main.rs[0m — menu loop and the eleven command handlers, with validation in the original order.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Behaviours worth flagging, all preserved deliberately:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mwhile (getchar() != '\n');[0m spins forever at EOF in the C, so the Rust does too. Both binaries hang identically (verified: same timeout exit code and same truncated output, since buffered stdout is lost on kill).[0m[0m
- Scene names and filenames are handled as raw bytes, so non-UTF-8 input round-trips exactly, including truncation at 63 bytes and the [38;5;10mstrcspn[0m newline trimming.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mscanf[0m overflow follows glibc's saturate-then-truncate-to-[38;5;10mint[0m behaviour, out-of-range shape types are silently skipped on load, and the "Scene is full" message stays on stderr.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One thing that cannot be reproduced faithfully: the C prints real heap addresses with [38;5;10m%p[0m in three places. Those vary per run under ASLR, so no output can match in general. I emulate glibc's allocation pattern — base plus a 0x9a0 stride, with the base adjusted for the terminal vs pipe stdout buffer size — which reproduces the reference binary exactly when run with randomization disabled ([38;5;10msetarch -R[0m), and that is how I verified those lines.[0m[0m
